Task: Build a dedicated native iOS “BlueMind Files” picker that visually belongs to the same design system as BlueMind Photos, while still being optimized for files rather than photos.

The goal is NOT to make Files identical to Photos.

The goal is:

same BlueMind design language

but

different content/layout appropriate for files.

Think of them as siblings:

BlueMind Photos

and

BlueMind Files

They should clearly look like they belong to the same app, with the same spacing philosophy, glass/material controls, rounded geometry, typography, accents, and interaction style.

⸻

DESIGN RELATIONSHIP TO BLUEMIND PHOTOS

Reuse the same overall design language already established in BlueMind Photos:

* native iOS dark material/glass;
* same corner-radius family;
* same header spacing;
* same X/close control style;
* same typography hierarchy;
* same BlueMind accent color;
* same native touch targets;
* same smooth iOS transitions;
* same bottom-sheet/fullscreen presentation style if appropriate.

Do NOT build a visually unrelated file browser.

A user should immediately feel:

“This is the Files section of the same BlueMind attachment system.”

⸻

BLUEMIND COLOR

If a primary accent is needed, use the existing official BlueMind color:

BLUE_MIND_COLORS.primary

CSS token:

--bm-primary

current value:

#193B68

Do not invent another blue.

Use it for:

* active tab;
* selected file;
* Add/Open action;
* focus states;
* small meaningful accents.

Do not make the whole interface blue.

⸻

HEADER

Use a header structurally similar to BlueMind Photos:

left:

* native iOS glass X / close

center:

* BlueMind Files

right:

* Add / Open / selected count if needed

If no file is selected, primary action can be disabled/subtle.

Example:

Add

or

Add (2)

depending on whether multi-select is supported in the current flow.

⸻

SEARCH

Add a native search field near the top.

Search should filter visible file names/available metadata when supported.

Keep its visual style consistent with the Photos tabs/search language.

Do not over-design it.

⸻

MAIN NAVIGATION

Files should not copy the Photos / Albums tabs literally.

Instead use file-appropriate sections such as:

* Recents
* Browse
* optionally Shared if supported safely by the current API.

These should feel like sibling tabs/segments to the Photos / Albums control in BlueMind Photos.

Same component family, different labels/content.

⸻

FILE LAYOUT

Unlike Photos, files do NOT need to be only a dense image grid.

Use the best layout for readability:

Prefer:

* a clean list;
* or adaptive grid/list toggle if genuinely useful.

Each file row/card should clearly show:

* file icon or preview;
* filename;
* file type;
* file size;
* modified date if available;
* iCloud/download state if applicable.

For PDFs:

* show a small first-page preview if efficiently available.

For Word/PowerPoint/Excel/TXT:

* use native/system file-type icons or lightweight previews.

Do not generate expensive previews for every file if it hurts performance.

⸻

VISUAL CONSISTENCY

Use the same visual rhythm as BlueMind Photos:

* similar section spacing;
* similar glass container styling;
* similar selected-state treatment;
* same icon weight;
* same text sizes/weights where hierarchy matches;
* same animations.

But Files should still feel more structured and document-oriented.

Photos = visual grid.

Files = document browser/list.

They should NOT look identical.

⸻

SELECTION STATE

When a file is selected:

* subtle rounded highlight;
* BlueMind primary accent where appropriate;
* clear checkmark or selected indicator;
* preserve native iOS feel.

Do not use loud custom colors.

⸻

FILE TYPES

Support the file formats BlueMind already accepts.

At minimum inspect existing logic for:

* PDF
* TXT
* DOC / DOCX
* PPT / PPTX
* XLS / XLSX
* image files
* any other existing supported attachment types.

Do NOT arbitrarily enable unsupported types just because they appear in Files.

Use the current attachment/backend capabilities as the source of truth.

⸻

IOS SECURITY / FILE ACCESS

This is important.

Do not bypass native iOS document permissions.

Inspect whether the current flow uses:

* UIDocumentPickerViewController
* security-scoped URLs
* Files provider access
* iCloud Drive

Preserve proper iOS access rules.

If a fully custom browser cannot safely replace a native document picker for certain sources, use a hybrid approach:

* BlueMind custom UI for recents/known accessible files where possible;
* native system picker where required for secure external locations.

Do NOT fake access to files BlueMind cannot actually read.

⸻

ICLOUD / REMOTE FILES

If a file exists only in iCloud:

* show that state clearly;
* allow native download/resolution;
* show loading progress/state;
* do not freeze indefinitely;
* handle failure gracefully.

⸻

ADD TO COMPOSER

When the user confirms a file:

* close BlueMind Files;
* return to chat composer;
* add exactly one attachment entry per selected file;
* no duplicates;
* preserve file name/type/size;
* show an immediate attachment card in composer;
* background-load additional metadata/preview if needed.

Do not make the user wait for unnecessary processing before the file appears in the composer.

⸻

PERFORMANCE

Keep scrolling smooth.

Avoid:

* reading entire files just to show the list;
* loading full PDFs/documents on the main thread;
* expensive preview generation for every offscreen item;
* base64 conversion for browsing;
* duplicate file reads.

Use lazy loading/caching where appropriate.

⸻

OPEN / CLOSE BEHAVIOR

Match BlueMind Photos:

* native iOS presentation;
* smooth open;
* smooth dismiss;
* X closes immediately;
* tap/gesture behavior should feel consistent.

⸻

DO NOT CHANGE

Do not modify:

* BlueMind Photos behavior;
* BlueMind Camera behavior;
* Web file picker behavior;
* backend;
* unrelated chat UI;
* voice/transcription.

This is an iOS attachment feature.

⸻

FIRST IMPLEMENTATION STEP

Before building the UI, inspect the current iOS file attachment path and report:

1. Which native iOS APIs are currently used.
2. Which file types are currently accepted.
3. Whether the current picker relies on UIDocumentPickerViewController.
4. Which parts can be safely replaced/customized.
5. Which parts should remain native for security-scoped access.

Then build the BlueMind Files presentation around that architecture.

⸻

FINAL TARGET

I want this attachment family:

BlueMind Camera

BlueMind Photos

BlueMind Files

All three should feel like one coherent BlueMind system.

Photos and Files should NOT be visually identical, but they should clearly share the same:

* brand;
* materials;
* spacing;
* controls;
* animations;
* accent color;
* interaction philosophy.

After implementation, report:

1. Which files/components changed.
2. Which native iOS document APIs are used.
3. How the Files UI reuses BlueMind Photos design tokens/components.
4. How file access/security is preserved.
5. How iCloud files are handled.
6. How files enter the composer without duplicates.
7. Confirmation that Web behavior was not modified.
